00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.400
PROFESSOR: If you
look at syllabus,

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.380
I have my office hour
Monday 5:00 to 6:00.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.580
And I would like to--

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.656
I know many of you, but also,
many of you I do not know.

00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:37.980
If we have not met
before, I would

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.990
like to ask you to
come to my office hour,

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.570
and let's chat a little bit
and see what your interest.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:49.230
That way, I can
better prepare myself.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:55.590
I also read that through your
feedback in the first class.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:59.160
As I expected, the
interest varies broadly.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:04.470
And also, the background
varies broadly.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:08.310
That's always the nature
I encounter in the past,

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.770
and we don't have a
good solution for that.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.410
So I ask, if you have learned
this stuff, be patient.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:20.760
If you haven't, we'll get there.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.770
And many of you, also, are
very much interested in liquid.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:32.670
So I want to tell you that
liquid is very difficult.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:39.150
And when I wrote the book, I put
everything on a parallel paths.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.540
I can deal with solid--

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.270
crystal solid,
crystalline solid--

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.820
and gas-- photons, electrons.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:53.440
But when it came to liquid, it
became a lot more complicated.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.350
So if you look at
the book, there

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.840
was a specific
chapter on liquid.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.700
And then, you have
to be more patient

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.240
and wait until almost
the end of this class.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.580
OK, with that, remember,
I would like to see you.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.800
If we haven't talked before,
come to my office hour.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.120
And let's know
each other better.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:24.750
If you think about what we
talked in the last lecture,

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:37.710
last time we reviewed the
classical laws for heat,

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.800
for momentum--

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.790
heat conduction, heat
convection, thermal radiation.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.910
In terms of momentum, for
example, the [INAUDIBLE] shear

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:49.410
stress [INAUDIBLE].

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:54.600
And we all saw Ohm's law,
mass diffusion, Fick's law.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.720
So those are the
constitute relations

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.860
for the transport processes.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.005
So the classical laws.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:18.480
And also, we went into
more microscopic picture

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.120
behind these classical laws.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.390
And we say, at the end,
in terms of carriers

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:28.260
for energy, momentum,
charge, we only

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.590
have essentially four types--

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:37.950
major types-- molecules,
electrons, photons,

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:39.960
and lattice vibration.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.020
I brought up the name phonons.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.190
So we went to some
microscopic description

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:56.890
for those transport processes.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:07.230
And what I simplify this
problem is a gas picture.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.200
I have molecular gas in a box.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:14.310
I have electron gas in a
box and the photon gas box.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:19.529
So that's why we can do a
parallel treatment for all

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:22.300
those energy carriers.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.970
And what I'm going to
do in today's lecture

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:31.260
is trying to give a first
crack on the questions

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.550
that we're going to answer
through the whole semester.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:42.490
And those questions-- what are
the questions I want to ask?

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:54.400
I want to ask, now, if I look
at those microscopic picture,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.690
I want to take, first,
one individual carrier

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.050
and ask, what's its energy?

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:04.960
I can also ask, what's
the momentum, charge?

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.130
But let me just pick
the energy first.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:14.230
And then I'm going to
look at a cluster, a box

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:18.430
of those carriers, molecules.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:25.240
At certain temperature, I ask,
how many of those carriers

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:31.060
have certain energy and
how fast they travel?

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.690
So first question is, how much?

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.030
OK, here can see
how much energy,

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.025
but there's one carrier.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.640
And then I'm going
to ask, how many

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:11.700
of them, and how
fast they travel,

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:19.485
and how far they travel,
and how they interact.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:34.320
OK.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:36.810
And with that, we can start--

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:41.880
I give you a simple derivation
of the diffusion laws.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:46.300
Last time, we showed
Ohm's law, for example.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:51.250
This time, I will show you the
Fourier heat conduction law.

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:55.150
And with that, you can go
back and do other laws--

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:58.270
the Fick's law and
Newton's shear stress law.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.930
These can all be similarly done.

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:07.600
So let's look at
per energy of per

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.595
carrier, so we
can say, how much?

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:19.010
Look at the one carrier.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:23.290
So I have a box
of gas molecules.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:35.110
Let's say, I have hydrogen. And
this is a diatomic molecule,

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:38.740
so we have two hydrogen atoms.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:42.310
And one hydrogen and
another hydrogen atom--

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:45.710
they are bound together.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:51.380
And then when I look at the
energy of this molecule,

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:57.350
I look at the possible modes,
possible ways of storing energy

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:00.510
by the molecule.

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:01.820
What other forms of energy--

00:08:05.103 --> 00:08:06.510
AUDIENCE: Kinetic and potential.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:07.570
PROFESSOR: I'm sorry?

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.195
AUDIENCE: Kinetic
and potential energy.

00:08:09.195 --> 00:08:12.480
PROFESSOR: OK, so we
have kinetic energy.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:13.800
That's a translation.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.470
So this hydrogen molecule
is moving around, right?

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:25.840
And in that case, I
look at the mass center.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:28.420
I can say, OK, I don't
care about these two

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.700
individual atoms.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:36.070
They are just a cluster, and
they move at certain velocity.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:43.270
And the kinetic energy, E,
equals 1/2 mv squared, right?

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:46.530
So last time I made a statement
if you understand energy,

00:08:46.530 --> 00:08:49.390
and momentum, and entropy.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:52.540
And now we're looking-- go
into the molecular picture

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.580
and look at those energy.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.350
So this is a translation--
kinetic energy.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.922
What other energies?

00:08:59.922 --> 00:09:01.672
AUDIENCE: Rotational
and vibrational.

00:09:01.672 --> 00:09:04.050
PROFESSOR: Rotation, vibration.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:07.810
And let's look at the vibration.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:13.170
So these two molecules
are bound by spring.

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:20.670
We, in fact, talked about
the inter-atomic force

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:21.930
in the last lecture.

00:09:21.930 --> 00:09:24.490
The minimum point
is the equilibrium.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:27.660
So we can approximate
as a spring.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:34.290
And this is a relative vibration
within these two atoms.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:36.960
That's another way
to store the energy.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:48.390
So the vibration-- and in fact,
that you can simplify this

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.970
because this now is
the two atom motion.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:55.110
But if you simplify it relative
to the center of the mass,

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:59.980
you can essentially do an
effective mass spring problem.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:03.930
So this is a classical
mass spring problem.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:11.028
And for this classical spring
mass, what's the energy?

00:10:11.028 --> 00:10:16.635
Energy-- let's say you
have the displacement is x.

00:10:16.635 --> 00:10:17.850
AUDIENCE: 1/2kx squared.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.110
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.750
1/2kx squared.

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:23.330
That's the potential.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.080
There's another part.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.170
One translation,
also, mv squared.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:35.790
This is a way--

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:40.980
now the center of mass moving,
it's a relative vibration,

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:43.370
so the mass is
relative to the center.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.320
AUDIENCE: In terms of the
potential energy, or--

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.190
PROFESSOR: This is
the potential energy--

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.417
kx squared.

00:10:50.417 --> 00:10:52.125
AUDIENCE: OK, then
those potential energy

00:10:52.125 --> 00:10:55.313
would start to [INAUDIBLE]
gravitational potential energy?

00:10:55.313 --> 00:10:55.980
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.240
I'm not considering gravity.

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:58.680
You can add a gravitation.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:02.370
Yeah, that's possible.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:10.800
OK, so we have vibration, and
you also mentioned rotation.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.090
So we have, also, rotation.

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:22.940
And rotation is, now, I
can take a simple picture.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:28.040
I fix this spring,
and now I have

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:34.950
a rod with two balls attached
to it, and they will rotate.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:41.820
And the energy-- rotational
energy is 1/2 i omega squared.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:54.760
i is the moment of inertia, and
omega is the angular momentum--

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.080
the angular velocity.

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:03.070
So we have translation,
vibration, rotation.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:03.880
Anything else?

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.060
Anything else?

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:13.405
Don't forget a charge.

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.640
So if I look at
individual hydrogen atom

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:33.560
and it has nuclei, and it
has electrons moving around.

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.290
So we also have electronic.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:48.780
And the electronic energy, I can
write a-- either use E symbol

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:50.580
or potential--

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.950
the interacting of two charge.

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:55.060
Let's say here is q1.

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:55.560
Here is q2.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.970
Then the cooling potential--

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.650
the cooling interaction,
two charges.

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:06.670
Anyone remember that?

00:13:10.780 --> 00:13:15.360
q1 and q2, 4 pi epsilon r.

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.610
Epsilon is dielectric constant.

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.400
r is the separation
between the two.

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:29.040
OK, so those are
the form of energy

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.900
this molecule stores within.

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:37.200
So when we think about
internal energy of a gas,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.470
they are stored in those
forms-- translation, vibration,

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:41.790
rotation, electronic.

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:52.450
And so that's the
case of a molecule.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:59.010
And if we go to solid, which
is a lot more complex--

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:01.660
we'll talk more later.

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:05.070
But the last time, we
gave a simple picture

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:09.480
of connected the atom by
spring, so it has a vibration.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:18.940
And also, each atom has
electrons and nuclei,

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.150
so it has the electronic.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:33.010
And in fact, for solid, it
doesn't have this rotation.

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.830
The atoms don't rotate.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:37.670
It's fixed there.

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:41.030
So in that sense, that's better.

00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:42.940
When I deal with
the liquid, I have

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.880
more trouble because of all
those other possibilities.

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:53.290
So let's leave this to later.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:59.140
And what I want to say is, those
are all the classical form,

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:02.810
classical expressions.

00:15:02.810 --> 00:15:05.540
And the what we're
going to move on next

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:09.260
is to give the quantized form.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:15.590
In quantum mechanics,
what are the energy

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:22.580
of the translation, vibration,
rotation, and electronic?

00:15:22.580 --> 00:15:26.760
And there, we'll say the
quantization of energy levels.

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:28.010
And that's very important.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:36.020
Now, this is a
individual energy--

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.090
how much?

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:44.560
What do we look next
is, how many of them?

00:15:54.560 --> 00:16:00.540
And I have a box
of molecules, here.

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:04.140
And of course, I can say,
well, per unit volume,

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:14.075
this is the number of
molecules per unit volume.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:24.510
That's the number of
molecules per unit volume.

00:16:24.510 --> 00:16:29.880
But each molecule could
have one half of mv squared.

00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.320
Now, v, in my current
picture, is not defined--

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.870
velocity.

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.690
It could be anywhere
from negative infinite

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:42.420
velocity to vector,
negative infinite

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:44.780
to positive and infinite.

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:47.840
So the question
is, if I give you

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:51.590
a box of gas at certain
temperature, T--

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.630
now I'm bringing
the thermal part--

00:16:54.630 --> 00:17:00.390
how much energy-- what's the
probability of the molecule

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:04.060
has certain value of energy, E?

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:12.050
Because the number of
molecules is very large,

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:14.330
I have to bring in the
statistical picture.

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:32.980
So at temperature T,
temperature T is--

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:37.240
let's look at the
translation only.

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:43.540
So I can simplify my picture
even more simplified.

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:46.270
I don't consider hydrogen atom.

00:17:46.270 --> 00:17:49.920
I consider helium atom.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:53.680
Monatomic gas--
translation energy only.

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:55.650
Of course, there's
electronic part.

00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:58.990
So at temperature
T, how much is--

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:04.150
say, what's the
probability of a molecule

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:06.625
has, actually, certain
value of energy?

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:12.080
What's the answer?

00:18:12.080 --> 00:18:14.266
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:16.703 --> 00:18:17.370
PROFESSOR: Yeah.

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:23.940
So this is going into the
statistical thermal physics--

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.390
statistical physics.

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:33.575
And the distribution is given
by the famous Boltzmann factor.

00:18:38.020 --> 00:18:39.730
I try to use all capitals, now.

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:56.370
So Boltzmann factor
tells you the probability

00:18:56.370 --> 00:19:00.690
of finding a
molecule or atom has

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:06.792
certain energy, E,
at temperature, T,

00:19:06.792 --> 00:19:09.670
is a normalization.

00:19:09.670 --> 00:19:10.920
This is a probability.

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:17.020
So it has a normalization
constant minus E over kBT.

00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:22.730
And this kB is the
Boltzmann constant

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:31.110
we mentioned last time. kB
is a 1.38 10 to the minus 23

00:19:31.110 --> 00:19:33.200
joule per Kelvin.

00:19:37.180 --> 00:19:40.150
So that's the Boltzmann factor.

00:19:40.150 --> 00:19:43.600
And some of you taken
statistical physics,

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:44.740
and that's easy.

00:19:44.740 --> 00:19:48.430
If you haven't taken it,
you learned [INAUDIBLE] law.

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:52.480
Any time people look at that
the temperature dependent,

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:55.060
they always like to pull
that exponential factor.

00:19:55.060 --> 00:19:59.700
That's because the
Boltzmann is behind it.

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:03.850
And then we'll come
back to this later on.

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:09.030
So I'm going to build on
this exponential factor

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:14.010
to determine, what's the
average energy per molecule--

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:18.310
translation energy per
molecule, and how to do it?

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:23.801
First, I need to find this
normalization factor, A,

00:20:23.801 --> 00:20:26.540
and then I determine
this probability.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:32.650
So let's examine the
translational energy--

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:33.670
mv squared.

00:20:33.670 --> 00:20:37.640
So v really is a vector.

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:39.280
So vx, vy, vz.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:44.895
So I'll write, vx squared plus
vy squared plus vz squared.

00:20:50.240 --> 00:20:55.490
And now, I take advantage
of the definition

00:20:55.490 --> 00:21:00.980
for this f probability is
I find the molecule having

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:04.400
velocity from minus
infinite to plus infinite.

00:21:04.400 --> 00:21:07.280
Mass will normalize to what?

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:09.570
That's all the
range you can have.

00:21:09.570 --> 00:21:13.770
So from the normalization--

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:19.500
from minus infinite to plus
infinite-- for vx, vy, vz--

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:22.680
first, I'll do it in
Cartesian coordinate,

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:26.610
then I'll transfer to
spherical coordinate.

00:21:26.610 --> 00:21:28.470
So let's look at,
first, Cartesian

00:21:28.470 --> 00:21:35.430
coordinate from minus
infinite to plus infinite dvx,

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:40.950
minus infinite to
plus infinite dvy.

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:48.600
And one warning is
that you will often

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:52.770
encounter many of
these integral forms

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:55.840
because when we look
at the probability,

00:21:55.840 --> 00:22:00.700
we have to integrate from
all the possible states.

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.480
So vx, vy, vz.

00:22:03.480 --> 00:22:07.320
And f-- so I put the
Boltzmann factor.

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.980
So this integrant is--

00:22:10.980 --> 00:22:12.580
I'm going to be lazy.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:17.370
I'm not writing that exponential
E, T. So you put it in there.

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:19.170
This should equal to 1.

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:24.260
You look at it.

00:22:24.260 --> 00:22:29.420
If you plug in your
E in vx squared,

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:33.830
vy squared, vz squared,
and you can factor it out

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:38.300
and you can do your integration
for dvx individually, dvy

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.310
individually, dvz individually.

00:22:41.310 --> 00:22:46.550
And with that, you only have
a constant A you do not know,

00:22:46.550 --> 00:22:50.470
and you determine
that constant A.

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:53.650
So I'm now going
through the integral.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:56.380
And you can either check
the integration table,

00:22:56.380 --> 00:22:58.900
or there is a nice
mathematical trick.

00:22:58.900 --> 00:23:03.590
If you like to play, you can
find that integration value.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:11.830
So here, what I have
is 2 pi kBT 3/2.

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:14.860
3/2 is because each of
this contributes 1/2.

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:21.980
OK So now, I have--

00:23:21.980 --> 00:23:33.590
if I plug in this A into the f,
I get f in terms of vx, vy, vz.

00:23:33.590 --> 00:23:36.620
The distribution
at temperature T

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:46.250
is m, 2 pi kBT, 3/2 exponential.

00:23:46.250 --> 00:23:48.560
I want to write the whole thing.

00:23:48.560 --> 00:23:55.610
So this is vx squared plus vy
squared plus vz squared divide

00:23:55.610 --> 00:23:57.150
by 2 kBT.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:02.150
And this is the
Maxwell distribution--

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:11.420
Maxwell velocity distribution.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.250
So it looks pretty tedious.

00:24:27.250 --> 00:24:29.040
But essentially, it's 1/2--

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:30.750
it's an energy expression--

00:24:30.750 --> 00:24:35.170
1/2 mv squared.

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:40.697
Maxwell-- what have you
heard about Maxwell?

00:24:40.697 --> 00:24:42.655
What other things have
you heard about Maxwell?

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:51.540
Maxwell equations-- you probably
heard that rather than this.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:57.570
Let's say, in fact, you look
at all those very famous people

00:24:57.570 --> 00:25:01.740
in the 1800s, 1900.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:04.060
They have done many things.

00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:06.780
Newton-- not just the
Newton mechanics law.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:09.530
Newton optics, also.

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:12.330
You can do all that.

00:25:12.330 --> 00:25:15.030
OK, here is velocity.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:21.570
Using vx, vy, vz, I could make
my life much easier if I use--

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:23.760
very often when I
do this integration,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.260
I change the coordinates.

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:29.160
Rather than dvx, dvy, dvz,
I do a spherical coordinate

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:32.410
because it's symmetric.

00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:40.420
so if I change my
coordinate from a dvx, dvy,

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:46.180
dvz into a spherical
coordinate, due to symmetry,

00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:48.280
it's 4 pi v squared dv.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.280
V, now, is they r--

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:55.030
the radius.

00:25:55.030 --> 00:25:58.950
And so the range of v is
from 0 to positive infinite.

00:25:58.950 --> 00:26:02.040
It's not from negative
infinite to infinite, right?

00:26:02.040 --> 00:26:06.330
When you do-- if you think
about from dx, dy, dz into r

00:26:06.330 --> 00:26:08.610
coordinate.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:14.040
OK, so because of that
my normalization-- when

00:26:14.040 --> 00:26:17.280
I do normalization, if I
use spherical coordinate,

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:20.120
it's 0 to infinite.

00:26:20.120 --> 00:26:25.070
And the fv, now I do
not use the vx, vy, vz.

00:26:25.070 --> 00:26:31.250
I use the fv and dv equals 1.

00:26:31.250 --> 00:26:34.280
That's another way to do it.

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:42.890
And in this case, your
fv equals 4 pi v squared,

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:54.733
m 2 pi kBT, 3/2 exponential
minus mv squared over 2kBT.

00:26:57.260 --> 00:27:04.340
It's simple rewriting the
Cartesian into a spherical.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:13.770
But the point I want to make is
if you use v, you do not do--

00:27:13.770 --> 00:27:17.050
because the 4 pi v squared
is already included here.

00:27:17.050 --> 00:27:20.130
So you can't substitute
this back into here

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:23.430
and just do your
vx, vy, vz, again.

00:27:23.430 --> 00:27:27.600
That's deconsistent
which coordinate system

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:30.900
you will be using.

00:27:30.900 --> 00:27:34.650
OK, so what does this
tell me, this math?

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:37.820
I'm jumping into
a little bit math.

00:27:37.820 --> 00:27:44.560
Because f is a probability,
so now with that probability,

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:47.620
I can calculate the average.

00:27:47.620 --> 00:27:52.300
I can calculate what's the
average energy per molecule,

00:27:52.300 --> 00:27:55.270
what's the average--

00:27:55.270 --> 00:27:59.010
any other numbers that
you will be interested.

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:06.540
So if I calculate the
average, E, per molecule--

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:12.090
the translation-- so my
average will be-- now,

00:28:12.090 --> 00:28:14.940
depend on which
system you choose.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:20.650
You can do Cartesian, or
you can do the spherical.

00:28:20.650 --> 00:28:25.300
Now, I'm going to write
simply as a spherical, so 0

00:28:25.300 --> 00:28:26.500
to infinite.

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:32.470
And the energy is
1/2 mv squared.

00:28:32.470 --> 00:28:35.560
That's the average quantity--
the quantity I want to find.

00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:42.200
What's the average it
given my f distribution?

00:28:42.200 --> 00:28:44.630
So I times fv.

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:50.130
I integrate the over v.

00:28:50.130 --> 00:28:55.640
Now you can do your math
to find out the integral.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:57.800
OK, that's why I'm
warning you, you

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:00.350
don't want to--
if you use v, you

00:29:00.350 --> 00:29:06.840
want to be consistently all
v. You don't do vx, vy, vz.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:09.150
If you do vx, vy, vz,
you start from here.

00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:11.220
You do your Cartesian
at the beginning.

00:29:11.220 --> 00:29:15.100
That f is different
from what I write here.

00:29:15.100 --> 00:29:18.070
It's the same thing, but
just to be consistency.

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:24.050
So again, I say, you
can do that homework.

00:29:24.050 --> 00:29:28.490
And what you have
here is 1/3 kBT.

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:35.390
All that math gives you a very
beautiful, very simple result.

00:29:35.390 --> 00:29:42.180
So kBT is a quantity
you will see often

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.070
if you go to microscopic work.

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:46.420
That's the thermal energy--

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:49.870
average thermal energy,
translation energy

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:51.490
of the molecule.

00:29:55.850 --> 00:29:56.815
Yes.

00:29:56.815 --> 00:30:02.743
AUDIENCE: So do not divide by
the integral from 0 to 3 of 1/2

00:30:02.743 --> 00:30:03.570
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:03.570 --> 00:30:07.050
PROFESSOR: If you divide
by this, that's 1.

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.250
AUDIENCE: Oh, OK.

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:09.870
PROFESSOR: So I already--

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:13.850
that's why my
normalization factor--

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:16.620
we're doing the
normalization factor.

00:30:16.620 --> 00:30:20.860
OK, so with this,
I can go to say,

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:22.470
what's the average velocity?

00:30:22.470 --> 00:30:27.480
I say, how much here is--
how much the average energy

00:30:27.480 --> 00:30:28.770
per particle?

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:32.490
Let's say, based on this,
because of this translation,

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:39.520
1/2 mv squared, so what's the
average speed of the molecule?

00:30:39.520 --> 00:30:41.150
So we can go to calculate it.

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:56.975
So I have average 1/2mv
squared equals 2/3 kBT.

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:04.410
And v squared--

00:31:04.410 --> 00:31:11.070
I take a square root of that so
I can tell the magnitude is--

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:16.140
here I have 3kBT over m.

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:22.030
OK, what's the
order of magnitude?

00:31:22.030 --> 00:31:23.080
Let's put in numbers.

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:26.310
I always like to put in numbers.

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:34.060
I take a helium gas.

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:37.060
And helium, I can
count how many--

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:39.600
what's the mass?

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:46.660
Helium has two neutrons, two
protons, two electron masses--

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:50.080
two electron masses
are two thousandths--

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:52.560
1 over 2000 of the
mass of protons.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:56.080
I forget about
the electron mass.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:32:00.790
So it's 4 times proton mass.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:07.670
Proton mass-- 1/67 10
to the minus 31 kilo.

00:32:07.670 --> 00:32:10.930
I can only remember SI unit.

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:13.120
Not 31-- I'm sorry.

00:32:13.120 --> 00:32:15.140
That's 31 is the election--

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:15.640
27.

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:26.960
OK, so if I plug in this
mass, kBT is the 1.38 10

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:28.580
to the minus 23.

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:31.670
Temperature is 300 Kelvin.

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:35.510
So what I'll get is
average velocity--

00:32:35.510 --> 00:32:44.465
speed, this one is 1.36 10 to
the third meter per second.

00:32:44.465 --> 00:32:48.590
It's about 1,000
meters per second.

00:32:48.590 --> 00:32:49.090
Yes.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:52.480
AUDIENCE: Do you know what the
variance is for the velocity?

00:32:52.480 --> 00:32:54.580
PROFESSOR: You could
go to calculate.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:59.590
You can calculate the--

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:01.120
it's also kBT.

00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:08.820
It's v minus v squared.

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:09.970
Do that.

00:33:09.970 --> 00:33:11.250
You'll get also kBT.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:13.470
That's the variance.

00:33:13.470 --> 00:33:16.746
OK, so this is helium.

00:33:16.746 --> 00:33:18.800
Now, if you put air--

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:24.660
air average mass 27.

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:29.620
So you put in 27, you'll get
about 500 meter per second.

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:34.900
Those molecules are
moving pretty fast.

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:39.180
And the last lecture, we
discussed the momentum,

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:41.690
the pressure.

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:44.470
We're feeling 1
atmospheric pressure,

00:33:44.470 --> 00:33:47.950
and that's the momentum from
these fast-moving molecules.

00:33:52.920 --> 00:33:56.250
So one more comment--

00:33:56.250 --> 00:34:06.240
and this 3/2 kBT, there is
a very fundamental theory

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:08.190
behind this.

00:34:08.190 --> 00:34:12.570
And that is if the
distribution of base--

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:19.300
the Boltzmann distribution--
and at high temperature--

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:22.350
so by doing that
high temperature,

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:23.760
I can do the integration.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:25.620
Later on, when we
introduce quantum,

00:34:25.620 --> 00:34:28.770
you can't do the
integration anymore.

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:31.440
So at high temperature,
the energy very close,

00:34:31.440 --> 00:34:33.880
so I can do integration.

00:34:33.880 --> 00:34:35.580
And if the energy form--

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:42.020
for each energy form,
if it's quadratic--

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:43.650
so what do I mean?

00:34:43.650 --> 00:34:47.130
So if you look at
the kinetic energy,

00:34:47.130 --> 00:34:50.239
this is 1/2 mv x squared, right?

00:34:50.239 --> 00:34:55.489
So each quadratic form, if you
go to do the average energy,

00:34:55.489 --> 00:34:57.980
you find that average
energy is 1/2 kT.

00:35:01.830 --> 00:35:07.590
So 1/2 kT, 1/2
kT, that's 3/2 kT.

00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:09.795
And that's called the
equipartition theorem.

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:35.880
Again, that's an example of
the translational energy.

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:42.960
And if you go to
vibration, you see here,

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:45.450
it's a quadratic term--

00:35:45.450 --> 00:35:47.910
1/2 kx squared.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:50.430
The velocity-- 1/2 mv squared.

00:35:50.430 --> 00:35:54.420
So at very high
temperature, this is 1/2 kT,

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:59.190
averagely speaking,
this is 1/2 kT.

00:35:59.190 --> 00:36:01.590
And the rotation--

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:05.440
1/2 omega squared.

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:08.200
Against, it's quadratic.

00:36:08.200 --> 00:36:12.990
At high temperature,
that's 1/2 kT.

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:15.120
And in fact, the rotation
at high temperature

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:17.730
is only 80 Kelvin
or so for molecules.

00:36:17.730 --> 00:36:22.210
The vibration is go to
a few thousand degree.

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:28.840
So that's why you learn
the ideal gas law before.

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:34.330
cv for different monotonic
ideal gas, diatomic ideal gas--

00:36:34.330 --> 00:36:36.940
they have different cv values.

00:36:36.940 --> 00:36:41.880
That's because diatomic gas,
you have the rotational modes

00:36:41.880 --> 00:36:43.400
coming in.

00:36:43.400 --> 00:36:48.150
And monotonic, only
the translation.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:51.370
That's the
equipartition theorem.

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:53.900
Well, we'll go to discuss
that more later on.

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:58.240
But here, with that, we've
got to fill in how fast

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:02.470
and how much energy they have.

00:37:02.470 --> 00:37:07.210
Next, what do we want to
answer is how far they travel.

00:37:13.740 --> 00:37:15.210
OK, so we have--

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:35.460
how far they travel.

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:42.680
By how far, I mean think about
the air molecules in this room.

00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:46.100
We have huge number.

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:49.000
Always, I give
numbers 10 to 23rd--

00:37:49.000 --> 00:37:50.870
one more.

00:37:50.870 --> 00:37:54.400
Huge number of
molecules, and they

00:37:54.400 --> 00:37:57.220
will collide with each other.

00:37:57.220 --> 00:38:01.440
So normally they carry their
energy, and then the next time,

00:38:01.440 --> 00:38:03.880
they collide with
another molecule.

00:38:03.880 --> 00:38:09.330
So I want to give a very
quick estimation, what's

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:12.380
the distance between
those collision?

00:38:12.380 --> 00:38:15.200
How far they travel?

00:38:15.200 --> 00:38:20.430
And so to do that,
let's say, in this case,

00:38:20.430 --> 00:38:25.430
I have to think about the
molecule along a point, right?

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:29.630
I have to think about the
final size of the molecule.

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:33.290
Its final size-- if you think
what determines the size,

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:35.990
it could be the electric
field potential.

00:38:35.990 --> 00:38:38.000
You have a nuclei,
and then there's

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:41.010
a electric field spill out.

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:45.350
And then when the two molecules
collide with each other,

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.075
that's when the fields
start to interact.

00:38:50.820 --> 00:39:02.060
And so I give, let's say, a
diameter of a molecule as D.

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:07.800
This diameter could be,
typically, a few angstroms--

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:11.040
1 angstrom, 2 angstroms.

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:13.060
OK.

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:19.780
And now, if I think about
this molecule final size,

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:20.610
it's moving.

00:39:20.610 --> 00:39:27.650
And the collision happens
when there is another molecule

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:32.270
within distance of D, right?

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:33.860
So this is 2D.

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:44.340
And the way I can think about it
and do a very quick estimation

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:50.520
is, let's say I have a molecule,
and over a trajectory--

00:39:53.440 --> 00:39:55.450
2-- of diameter 2D--

00:39:55.450 --> 00:39:58.250
it travel a certain distance.

00:39:58.250 --> 00:40:02.860
So this tube is diameter 2D.

00:40:02.860 --> 00:40:05.020
And travel a certain
distance-- let's say,

00:40:05.020 --> 00:40:07.950
here, this distance is L.

00:40:07.950 --> 00:40:10.020
And then, what's the volume?

00:40:10.020 --> 00:40:17.250
So that tube is the
area times distance.

00:40:17.250 --> 00:40:26.600
So the volume is
4 pi 2D squared--

00:40:30.980 --> 00:40:33.230
this is the area--

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:39.050
times the length
gives me the volume

00:40:39.050 --> 00:40:42.320
that this molecule,
this tube sweeps.

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:56.230
Now, how I decide the average
distance between 1 collision,

00:40:56.230 --> 00:40:59.900
that is when the number
density per unit volume--

00:40:59.900 --> 00:41:03.580
this is the number of
molecules per unit volume--

00:41:03.580 --> 00:41:06.640
times the volume--
when that equals 1.

00:41:06.640 --> 00:41:09.430
That's one collision.

00:41:09.430 --> 00:41:11.330
Do you agree?

00:41:11.330 --> 00:41:15.680
So when the average number
of, say, the per unit volume--

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:21.050
what's the number of molecule--
times over this tube of 2D,

00:41:21.050 --> 00:41:25.040
the length, L,
when this equals 1,

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:27.860
that's one collision
this molecule

00:41:27.860 --> 00:41:31.210
will have with other molecules.

00:41:31.210 --> 00:41:36.460
And under this one
condition that L is really

00:41:36.460 --> 00:41:39.310
what I call the mean free
path, the average distance

00:41:39.310 --> 00:41:44.380
between collision,
equals, here, you

00:41:44.380 --> 00:41:55.120
can say is pi D squared times n.

00:41:55.120 --> 00:41:59.310
This is the mean free
path that the molecule

00:41:59.310 --> 00:42:01.713
travels between collision.

00:42:07.180 --> 00:42:10.720
And if you do a
better job, this one

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:14.440
is assuming the other
molecules do not move--

00:42:14.440 --> 00:42:17.470
just a stationary
distribute there.

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:20.980
And if you do more
detail, it turns out

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:23.570
this expression is
still pretty good.

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:24.910
So the mean free path--

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:40.180
mean free path is--

00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:42.670
more accurate that is
put the square root of 2

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:45.749
here is pi D squared times n.

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:52.820
Now with that, we
can go to guess

00:42:52.820 --> 00:42:58.750
estimate the mean free
path of the air molecules--

00:42:58.750 --> 00:43:00.600
a typical gas.

00:43:00.600 --> 00:43:02.690
So if it's a gas--

00:43:02.690 --> 00:43:03.455
ideal gas.

00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:13.320
If it's ideal gas and
we know pressure--

00:43:13.320 --> 00:43:20.440
we derived last time,
in fact-- is nkBT,

00:43:20.440 --> 00:43:24.505
n is the number of particles
per unit volume, nothing more--

00:43:24.505 --> 00:43:25.005
fraction.

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:29.950
So this is number of
particles per unit volume.

00:43:29.950 --> 00:43:31.770
So I replaced this n.

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:37.680
And in this case, what I
get the mean free path is

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:44.820
kBT, square root of 2,
pi D squared pressure.

00:43:48.420 --> 00:43:51.730
OK, so let's put it
in number and m what's

00:43:51.730 --> 00:43:56.910
the mean free path in
a typical condition?

00:43:56.910 --> 00:44:10.630
Gamma is-- kBT is kB is 1.38 10
to the minus 23 joules Kelvin

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:14.200
times 300 Kelvin.

00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:19.720
And square root of 2 pi D.

00:44:19.720 --> 00:44:24.410
D let me take 2.5 angstrom--

00:44:24.410 --> 00:44:28.010
this is by no means
meant to be accurate--

00:44:28.010 --> 00:44:29.150
meter squared.

00:44:32.950 --> 00:44:43.920
Pressure-- 1 atmosphere, so 110
to the fifth Newton per meter

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:45.880
squared.

00:44:45.880 --> 00:44:50.110
And you go to put in all the
number, so this is minus 23rd.

00:44:50.110 --> 00:44:54.350
This is minus 20.

00:44:54.350 --> 00:44:59.960
And here-- so that means
the 20-- minus 20--

00:44:59.960 --> 00:45:00.860
minus 3.

00:45:00.860 --> 00:45:02.750
And here is 10 the fifth.

00:45:02.750 --> 00:45:05.210
So minus 8.

00:45:05.210 --> 00:45:09.770
And this 300-- you go
to check your numbers.

00:45:09.770 --> 00:45:11.390
I'll give you a value I have.

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:20.280
This one I get 0.14.

00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:28.716
So 0.14 micron-- so 10 to
the minus 600 nanometer.

00:45:28.716 --> 00:45:31.540
That's about the
average distance

00:45:31.540 --> 00:45:33.850
the air molecules
travel in this room.

00:45:38.430 --> 00:45:44.270
And if you recall, before
I said a disk drive--

00:45:44.270 --> 00:45:47.780
and the disk in my [INAUDIBLE]
is a few nanometer, now,

00:45:47.780 --> 00:45:48.980
the separation.

00:45:48.980 --> 00:45:50.870
And they're still
using fluid mechanics

00:45:50.870 --> 00:45:53.000
to design their slider
so that they do not

00:45:53.000 --> 00:45:54.930
collide with the disk.

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:57.570
And you come to a regime
where the air molecules do not

00:45:57.570 --> 00:45:59.820
collide with each other,
they'll collide with boundary

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:02.520
more often.

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:07.040
So this is the average
mean free pass.

00:46:07.040 --> 00:46:10.720
Now, you can say in
the case of ideal gas,

00:46:10.720 --> 00:46:14.610
the pressure is in
the denominator.

00:46:14.610 --> 00:46:19.860
How many of you do
vacuum-related, vacuum

00:46:19.860 --> 00:46:20.520
experience?

00:46:23.310 --> 00:46:29.810
So vacuum-- typical vacuum--
let's say, 10 to minus 6 torr.

00:46:29.810 --> 00:46:35.540
You can use a typical diffusion
pump to easily derive 10

00:46:35.540 --> 00:46:37.130
to minus 6 torr.

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:39.860
So what's the molecule
mean free pass

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:41.490
when it's 10 to minus 6 torr?

00:46:44.970 --> 00:46:50.590
I put in there 10 to minus
torr is 10 to the minus 6--

00:46:50.590 --> 00:46:54.490
remember, here is
10 to the fifth.

00:46:54.490 --> 00:46:57.880
But the one torr is 760--

00:46:57.880 --> 00:47:00.650
1 atmosphere is 760 torr.

00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:04.000
So this is the pressure
you put in there,

00:47:04.000 --> 00:47:05.650
and you get 100 meter.

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:10.470
So in vacuum pump--

00:47:10.470 --> 00:47:15.030
vacuum system-- the molecule
do not collide with each other.

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:17.630
You use a evaporator.

00:47:17.630 --> 00:47:18.500
You heat it up.

00:47:18.500 --> 00:47:19.730
You heat the atom.

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:20.750
The atom go out.

00:47:20.750 --> 00:47:24.990
It goes straight to your target.

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:27.930
So it's completely a
ballistic trajectory.

00:47:27.930 --> 00:47:32.400
You don't do the heat induction
diffusion theory anymore.

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:35.490
You have to trace
the trajectory.

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:43.570
OK, so this is how far--

00:47:43.570 --> 00:47:45.790
we have some idea.

00:47:45.790 --> 00:47:51.670
And next, let's
briefly discuss how

00:47:51.670 --> 00:47:53.290
they interact with each other.

00:48:16.695 --> 00:48:17.570
How do they interact?

00:48:27.510 --> 00:48:33.720
And I mentioned when the
two molecules collided

00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:37.860
with each other it's
really the electric field

00:48:37.860 --> 00:48:42.720
extend certain space and
when the two molecules get

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:46.330
close, the electric field
collide, say, repel each other.

00:48:46.330 --> 00:48:49.260
So they go different trajectory.

00:48:49.260 --> 00:48:53.940
So you have simple--

00:48:53.940 --> 00:48:58.180
you can think about that
two billiard ball collision.

00:48:58.180 --> 00:49:02.140
And if you want to do
the trajectory, what

00:49:02.140 --> 00:49:04.420
do you do is during
the coalition,

00:49:04.420 --> 00:49:06.580
you have energy conservation.

00:49:06.580 --> 00:49:09.400
You have momentum conservation.

00:49:09.400 --> 00:49:11.620
So in that case,
this is really--

00:49:11.620 --> 00:49:15.380
the microscopic world-- the
basic conservation laws,

00:49:15.380 --> 00:49:17.240
the conservation
principle apply.

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:25.514
So you have energy and
momentum conservation.

00:49:37.150 --> 00:49:46.610
And if the collision is
among the air molecules,

00:49:46.610 --> 00:49:48.995
typically, it's
limited how fast--

00:49:48.995 --> 00:49:51.020
how far the molecule travel.

00:49:51.020 --> 00:49:53.300
So it's an internal--

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:58.140
you can think that more as the
internal stuff, internal laws.

00:49:58.140 --> 00:50:01.250
But when you think about
the energy conversion, what

00:50:01.250 --> 00:50:03.200
does it really-- energy
conversion-- mean?

00:50:06.460 --> 00:50:13.640
Energy conversion is one type of
energy going to the other type,

00:50:13.640 --> 00:50:14.600
right?

00:50:14.600 --> 00:50:19.840
So when gas molecule
drive a turbine,

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:27.210
it's this kinetic energy
becomes the average motion

00:50:27.210 --> 00:50:28.010
of the blade.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:36.620
Now, another type is think
about the photovoltaic cell.

00:50:36.620 --> 00:50:38.390
What's the energy conversion?

00:50:38.390 --> 00:50:42.020
Of course, what you want is
the photon eventually come out

00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:44.480
and get the electricity, right?

00:50:44.480 --> 00:50:47.330
But what you get in
the microscopic level

00:50:47.330 --> 00:50:50.880
is an electron is
sent over here.

00:50:50.880 --> 00:50:57.230
A photon comes in and
lift the electron to here.

00:50:57.230 --> 00:50:59.900
You have the first step of
your energy conversion already.

00:51:03.050 --> 00:51:06.440
Of course, that's not a
photovoltaic cell, yet,

00:51:06.440 --> 00:51:10.190
and the light from the lamp
is absorbed by all the stuff

00:51:10.190 --> 00:51:12.320
around us, eventually.

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:14.900
That's energy conversion,
except at the end,

00:51:14.900 --> 00:51:15.890
we can't capture it.

00:51:19.410 --> 00:51:25.410
And so you have different
losses in the process

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:29.820
that you have to take care if
you want to finally extract

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:33.000
things that's useful.

00:51:33.000 --> 00:51:38.550
OK, so this kind of interaction
is dealing with the collision,

00:51:38.550 --> 00:51:40.110
dealing with the interaction.

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:44.640
This is arguably the
most complicated step

00:51:44.640 --> 00:51:45.914
in the transport.

00:51:49.990 --> 00:51:53.470
Now, with that,
let's go to what I

00:51:53.470 --> 00:52:00.880
want to give you is a simple
kinetic theory of transport.

00:52:14.590 --> 00:52:23.050
So I know their, say, how
long air molecule travel,

00:52:23.050 --> 00:52:25.570
and I know their velocity.

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:29.350
What I'm going to
do next is to show

00:52:29.350 --> 00:52:33.640
how we can do a simple
derivation for the Fourier

00:52:33.640 --> 00:52:35.470
law of heat conduction.

00:52:35.470 --> 00:52:38.440
And I said that you can do
the same derivation using

00:52:38.440 --> 00:52:41.680
the same strategy
for mass diffusion,

00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:44.710
for momentum transfer.

00:52:44.710 --> 00:52:49.750
And in fact, you have
homework on this.

00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:58.480
So I'm considering conduction
only now averaging motion,

00:52:58.480 --> 00:52:59.320
right?

00:52:59.320 --> 00:53:04.470
I have the gas
molecules and any gas.

00:53:04.470 --> 00:53:07.600
In fact, it applies to the
electron, applies to photon,

00:53:07.600 --> 00:53:09.520
as long as I have
this gas picture.

00:53:12.130 --> 00:53:16.250
And I have a temperature
gradient in the x-direction.

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:19.450
So this is a p flow
in the x-direction--

00:53:24.210 --> 00:53:27.080
temperature gradient there.

00:53:27.080 --> 00:53:32.200
And I want to derive
the Fourier law.

00:53:32.200 --> 00:53:36.750
And microscopically,
if I can count--

00:53:36.750 --> 00:53:38.730
basically, I'm counting.

00:53:38.730 --> 00:53:45.770
So when I look at the flux
across certain cross section--

00:53:45.770 --> 00:53:48.970
so here, I'm going to say
this is the cross section I'm

00:53:48.970 --> 00:53:49.810
looking at.

00:53:49.810 --> 00:53:52.070
Here is x.

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.160
On the left, I have molecules.

00:53:55.160 --> 00:53:58.810
On the right, I have molecules.

00:53:58.810 --> 00:54:01.450
And the molecules are
doing random motion.

00:54:04.250 --> 00:54:08.550
And on the left, they are
slight higher temperature.

00:54:08.550 --> 00:54:11.420
On the right, slightly
lower temperature.

00:54:11.420 --> 00:54:14.990
Now, some of these
molecule will go

00:54:14.990 --> 00:54:21.530
across this imaginary interface
I draw without collision.

00:54:21.530 --> 00:54:23.270
So I count that--

00:54:23.270 --> 00:54:26.160
those molecule that can
go through the interface.

00:54:26.160 --> 00:54:28.670
So from left to right,
mass from right to

00:54:28.670 --> 00:54:32.640
left-- that's my left flux.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:35.480
So I'm doing my energy balance.

00:54:35.480 --> 00:54:39.010
And so what I
count, I say, left I

00:54:39.010 --> 00:54:43.700
have here within how high--
say, what's the range?

00:54:43.700 --> 00:54:47.500
This is the vx times tau.

00:54:47.500 --> 00:54:51.880
Tau is the time
between collision.

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:54.190
That's something I
forgot to mention--

00:54:54.190 --> 00:54:54.700
tau.

00:54:54.700 --> 00:54:57.460
Because I have a
mean free path, so I

00:54:57.460 --> 00:55:01.450
have the mean free path divide
by velocity that gives me--

00:55:01.450 --> 00:55:06.860
or speed-- gives me the
average time between collision.

00:55:06.860 --> 00:55:08.870
So I say, vx--

00:55:08.870 --> 00:55:11.960
because this is velocity
in the x-component,

00:55:11.960 --> 00:55:15.810
in x-direction-- we
have x times tau.

00:55:15.810 --> 00:55:19.470
As long as those molecules
is within this range,

00:55:19.470 --> 00:55:23.130
they can go across this
imaginary interface here.

00:55:26.400 --> 00:55:29.190
And of course, only half
of them have that chance.

00:55:29.190 --> 00:55:30.210
Half goes this way.

00:55:30.210 --> 00:55:31.330
Half goes the other way.

00:55:31.330 --> 00:55:33.620
I'm doing the random--

00:55:33.620 --> 00:55:35.400
random Brownian motion.

00:55:35.400 --> 00:55:43.230
So I have my q is 1/2.

00:55:43.230 --> 00:55:49.200
This is the average number
per unit per unit volume.

00:55:49.200 --> 00:55:52.450
And times vx--
this is the speed.

00:55:52.450 --> 00:55:54.120
So if you look at
this, this gives

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.360
me here is per unit volume.

00:55:57.360 --> 00:55:59.020
Here is meter per second.

00:55:59.020 --> 00:56:02.760
So that gives me per
meter squared per second.

00:56:02.760 --> 00:56:05.330
That's how they flux--

00:56:05.330 --> 00:56:13.030
the flux of the particles
per meter squared per second.

00:56:13.030 --> 00:56:16.560
And times each particle
carry this energy.

00:56:16.560 --> 00:56:19.620
So you can replace
that energy by charge.

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:22.830
That's a diffusion of charge.

00:56:22.830 --> 00:56:25.960
If you do 1, that's
a diffusion--

00:56:25.960 --> 00:56:28.860
or if you times the
mass of a particle,

00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:34.530
that's a diffusion of mass
in terms of the weight.

00:56:34.530 --> 00:56:40.860
So here is x mass vx times tau.

00:56:40.860 --> 00:56:44.120
That's on the left
going into right.

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:47.850
And the mass from the
right going to left--

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:52.850
so there also half of this--
this is also vx over tau.

00:56:52.850 --> 00:56:56.710
That's the chance going
from left to the right.

00:56:56.710 --> 00:57:01.738
So that's the difference
of the two gives me--

00:57:01.738 --> 00:57:05.590
you say here that
it's vx plus tau.

00:57:10.010 --> 00:57:12.270
So that's my heat flux.

00:57:12.270 --> 00:57:18.120
If you check, this is a
1 over meter squared--

00:57:18.120 --> 00:57:19.080
cubic.

00:57:19.080 --> 00:57:21.510
Here is meter per second.

00:57:21.510 --> 00:57:23.430
Here is joule.

00:57:23.430 --> 00:57:25.620
So I will get a Watt
per meter squared.

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:32.790
And of course, what
I'm going to do next--

00:57:32.790 --> 00:57:35.070
oh, here is vx--

00:57:35.070 --> 00:57:36.390
x components.

00:57:36.390 --> 00:57:38.805
What I'm going to do
next is to write that

00:57:38.805 --> 00:57:39.960
into differentiable form.

00:57:39.960 --> 00:57:42.150
This is very short.

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:45.540
As long as I'm dealing
with microscopic,

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:51.790
I can write this 100-nanometer
range into differentiable form.

00:57:51.790 --> 00:57:53.220
So I do my differential.

00:58:03.220 --> 00:58:05.830
What I have is q equals--

00:58:05.830 --> 00:58:08.500
because this is a
positive, it's minus.

00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:10.780
So I have minus in the front--

00:58:10.780 --> 00:58:16.140
1/2 d nvx tau--

00:58:16.140 --> 00:58:19.570
E. That's what's inside.

00:58:19.570 --> 00:58:21.730
And dx.

00:58:21.730 --> 00:58:28.980
And the x difference
is vx tau mass vx tau.

00:58:28.980 --> 00:58:31.005
So I have 2 vx tau.

00:58:38.060 --> 00:58:43.630
So now I'm doing a
little [INAUDIBLE]..

00:58:43.630 --> 00:58:45.250
I'm going to put the vx--

00:58:45.250 --> 00:58:47.620
pull vx out.

00:58:47.620 --> 00:58:52.560
I neglect all those temperature
dependence of velocity.

00:58:52.560 --> 00:58:54.960
And this is actually because
you can have a [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:54.960 --> 00:58:56.730
the mass heat transfer.

00:58:56.730 --> 00:58:59.500
But I'm escaping-- making
the science easier.

00:58:59.500 --> 00:59:03.570
So what I have here
is one 1/2 cancel.

00:59:03.570 --> 00:59:15.627
I have vx squared tau and d
n times E is U and the dx.

00:59:19.780 --> 00:59:23.410
U is energy per unit volume.

00:59:27.170 --> 00:59:34.570
So vx squared is
1/3 of v squared.

00:59:34.570 --> 00:59:37.120
I'm doing-- right?

00:59:37.120 --> 00:59:40.330
So what I have here--
oh, the negative sign.

00:59:40.330 --> 00:59:48.130
So I have 1/3 v
squared tau and dU dT--

00:59:48.130 --> 00:59:50.170
I'm using chain rule--

00:59:50.170 --> 00:59:53.800
dT, dX.

00:59:53.800 --> 00:59:54.720
What's dU, dT?

00:59:57.230 --> 01:00:01.460
That's the heat-- yeah,
specific heat per unit volume.

01:00:01.460 --> 01:00:03.260
So this one is--

01:00:03.260 --> 01:00:09.500
c is the specific heat is
joule meter cubic Kelvin.

01:00:09.500 --> 01:00:12.320
Typically, we do specific
per unit density--

01:00:12.320 --> 01:00:13.550
per unit mass.

01:00:13.550 --> 01:00:15.120
But it's fine.

01:00:15.120 --> 01:00:19.970
So what I have here is
k, Fourier law, dT, dX.

01:00:19.970 --> 01:00:32.380
And I also obtain expression for
k is 1/3 cv tau squared, right?

01:00:32.380 --> 01:00:34.460
No, cv squared tau.

01:00:34.460 --> 01:00:41.630
cv squared tau, which
is 1/3 c gamma v--

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:43.560
cv gamma.

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:50.330
So this is the
Fourier law and also

01:00:50.330 --> 01:00:52.875
the expression for
thermal conductivity.

01:00:57.870 --> 01:00:59.420
OK.

01:00:59.420 --> 01:01:02.360
And if you look at
thermal connectivity,

01:01:02.360 --> 01:01:06.380
it's proportional to
the mean free path,

01:01:06.380 --> 01:01:11.060
proportional velocity,
proportional specific heat.

01:01:11.060 --> 01:01:15.030
If you check for gas--

01:01:15.030 --> 01:01:18.883
which gas has the highest
thermal conductivity?

01:01:18.883 --> 01:01:19.550
AUDIENCE: Helium

01:01:19.550 --> 01:01:21.410
PROFESSOR: Helium.

01:01:21.410 --> 01:01:23.695
So the velocity is higher.

01:01:28.420 --> 01:01:30.140
OK.

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:36.160
And so we have-- with that,
I have the simple derivation.

01:01:36.160 --> 01:01:38.140
And later on, we're
going to give you

01:01:38.140 --> 01:01:42.220
more complicated derivation
based on Boltzmann equation.

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:45.460
But this is a [INAUDIBLE]
one, and it's pretty good.

01:01:45.460 --> 01:01:47.050
At the end, if you
use the Boltzmann,

01:01:47.050 --> 01:01:50.350
you'll still get k
equals 1/3 of cv gamma.

01:01:54.600 --> 01:01:58.770
OK, any questions
here, or doubt?

01:02:02.634 --> 01:02:08.590
If not, let's make
a few comment.

01:02:08.590 --> 01:02:12.190
So those, of course,
are what we learned--

01:02:12.190 --> 01:02:16.660
the classical defusing
theory, right?

01:02:16.660 --> 01:02:20.070
And you can say
immediately, if I

01:02:20.070 --> 01:02:26.630
do take the example
of the disk drive,

01:02:26.630 --> 01:02:31.370
my space is much smaller
than this mean free path.

01:02:31.370 --> 01:02:33.800
I cannot do the
derivative anymore.

01:02:37.390 --> 01:02:41.960
So I have to go to look
at more complicated--

01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:46.160
so this is where the
nano start to deviate.

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:50.020
So what we're looking at when
we're looking into the, what's

01:02:50.020 --> 01:02:51.280
the fundamental difference?

01:02:57.680 --> 01:03:08.140
So I have-- and what's, really,
the micro/nano fundamental

01:03:08.140 --> 01:03:08.860
difference?

01:03:18.270 --> 01:03:20.830
And there are several regimes.

01:03:20.830 --> 01:03:25.920
One is I always have a
device characteristic length.

01:03:25.920 --> 01:03:29.070
For example, in the
slider, the disk drive

01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:33.120
is the separation
between slider and disk.

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:36.540
So I have one is a
characteristic length.

01:03:36.540 --> 01:03:37.950
The mean free path--

01:03:37.950 --> 01:03:41.520
if the mean free
path becomes larger

01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:46.230
or comparable to
separation-- let's say,

01:03:46.230 --> 01:03:51.450
d, a characteristic length
of the transport problem.

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:58.560
And in that case, the diffusion
theory will no longer be valid,

01:03:58.560 --> 01:04:02.740
and the air molecule will
collide with the boundary.

01:04:02.740 --> 01:04:07.700
And as long as the quantum
effects are not important,

01:04:07.700 --> 01:04:12.110
then you run into a
classical size effect regime.

01:04:12.110 --> 01:04:14.510
So classical size effect.

01:04:30.470 --> 01:04:34.040
So this is one case.

01:04:34.040 --> 01:04:37.820
But you can ask, how do you know
your quantum size effect is not

01:04:37.820 --> 01:04:38.736
important?

01:04:41.740 --> 01:04:43.250
So let's take a look.

01:04:43.250 --> 01:04:45.730
Now, we're going to move
a little bit into quantum.

01:04:52.500 --> 01:04:56.595
So the next case is a
quantum size effect.

01:05:10.650 --> 01:05:14.780
And in fact, sometimes I don't
like to call it a quantum size

01:05:14.780 --> 01:05:16.420
effect.

01:05:16.420 --> 01:05:18.415
I like to call it wave effect.

01:05:21.940 --> 01:05:24.310
And this is a case
when you have to think

01:05:24.310 --> 01:05:31.120
about the wavelength as another
characteristic than scale.

01:05:31.120 --> 01:05:37.135
And I prefer to call
this save effects.

01:05:43.060 --> 01:05:46.570
I'm sure that people don't
always agree with me.

01:05:46.570 --> 01:05:51.640
But say, here,
let's take a look.

01:05:51.640 --> 01:05:52.990
Plus, with the quantum--

01:05:52.990 --> 01:05:59.470
with the wave-- take
a basic picture here.

01:05:59.470 --> 01:06:05.500
We know we're going to get into
more in the next lecture that--

01:06:05.500 --> 01:06:08.950
see, anything from
quantum picture

01:06:08.950 --> 01:06:13.160
is both a wave and
the particle, right?

01:06:13.160 --> 01:06:16.660
So wave has a wavelength.

01:06:16.660 --> 01:06:20.060
So we have a wavelength.

01:06:20.060 --> 01:06:21.940
This is my lambda.

01:06:26.470 --> 01:06:31.090
And in the quantum
picture, again,

01:06:31.090 --> 01:06:34.720
say we're going
more in the lecture.

01:06:34.720 --> 01:06:39.940
The wavelength and the
momentum is related

01:06:39.940 --> 01:06:42.700
through the Planck constant, h.

01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:44.830
h is a Planck constant--

01:06:44.830 --> 01:06:50.605
6.6 10 to the minus
34 joule second.

01:06:55.680 --> 01:07:02.110
So now, when the wave's
effect important,

01:07:02.110 --> 01:07:06.440
I'm going to give a very
simple argument-- example.

01:07:06.440 --> 01:07:11.410
In fact, you will say that
a significant difference--

01:07:11.410 --> 01:07:15.430
even though we, in
most of my research

01:07:15.430 --> 01:07:19.060
is fall into classical
size effect regime,

01:07:19.060 --> 01:07:22.120
but I've been
always thinking it's

01:07:22.120 --> 01:07:24.580
much better if you start
with the wave picture

01:07:24.580 --> 01:07:31.400
to understand how the wave
goes from wave to the particle.

01:07:31.400 --> 01:07:32.150
Why?

01:07:32.150 --> 01:07:37.470
That's because when you go nano,
there is always the interface.

01:07:37.470 --> 01:07:42.390
Interface-- ideal interface--
is zero thickness.

01:07:42.390 --> 01:07:46.170
Zero thickness compare anything
is too thin, too small, right?

01:07:46.170 --> 01:07:49.110
So there, the wave
effect always--

01:07:49.110 --> 01:07:51.070
one can start with
think about the way

01:07:51.070 --> 01:07:54.280
reflection at the interface.

01:07:54.280 --> 01:08:00.510
So even though most
of you probably

01:08:00.510 --> 01:08:03.420
don't deal with
waves very often,

01:08:03.420 --> 01:08:06.030
I want to emphasize the
wave-- spend a lot of time

01:08:06.030 --> 01:08:09.360
talking about the waves.

01:08:09.360 --> 01:08:14.370
So let me make a simple
argument of standing wave.

01:08:14.370 --> 01:08:15.570
I have a film.

01:08:15.570 --> 01:08:19.479
Or in the first lecture, I
say, if you go to Professor

01:08:19.479 --> 01:08:23.880
[INAUDIBLE] website, you'll
see he makes beautiful dots--

01:08:23.880 --> 01:08:27.000
nano-sized dots.

01:08:27.000 --> 01:08:29.700
And so I simplify that.

01:08:29.700 --> 01:08:33.029
As a film, I make a
two-dimensional, very thin,

01:08:33.029 --> 01:08:34.710
freestanding film.

01:08:34.710 --> 01:08:36.215
I got the electron wave inside.

01:08:38.899 --> 01:08:41.470
I have a charger
inside, or I have atom.

01:08:41.470 --> 01:08:44.359
You can think of either way.

01:08:44.359 --> 01:08:47.229
So this is a wave inside.

01:08:47.229 --> 01:08:51.770
And then there is a boundary
you cannot come out.

01:08:51.770 --> 01:08:53.029
So what do you have there?

01:08:56.229 --> 01:09:01.500
So confine the boundary--
simplest picture is clamped.

01:09:01.500 --> 01:09:03.720
You say, OK, my wave--

01:09:03.720 --> 01:09:05.430
I thought that depends--

01:09:05.430 --> 01:09:08.170
I wasn't sure I'm
coming to this stage,

01:09:08.170 --> 01:09:11.848
so I didn't bring
the violin I have.

01:09:11.848 --> 01:09:13.140
AUDIENCE: It's a standing wave.

01:09:13.140 --> 01:09:14.970
PROFESSOR: It's a standing wave.

01:09:14.970 --> 01:09:18.392
So let's start with
standing wave picture.

01:09:18.392 --> 01:09:24.740
And I have a final thickness.

01:09:24.740 --> 01:09:28.670
Let me say my simplest
picture is standing wave

01:09:28.670 --> 01:09:32.060
with at the boundary
is zero vibration.

01:09:32.060 --> 01:09:35.600
I clamp these two boundaries.

01:09:35.600 --> 01:09:38.941
So then my waveform--

01:09:38.941 --> 01:09:42.160
this is a standing wave--

01:09:42.160 --> 01:09:46.178
the simplest one mode
of standing wave.

01:09:46.178 --> 01:09:46.970
And the next mode--

01:09:50.740 --> 01:09:52.270
right?

01:09:52.270 --> 01:09:55.720
So I have those different
modes of standing wave inside.

01:09:58.290 --> 01:10:02.020
And I can use the
expression I gave

01:10:02.020 --> 01:10:07.430
and this requirement to give
you basic quantization result.

01:10:07.430 --> 01:10:10.000
So how we do it?

01:10:10.000 --> 01:10:13.800
Lambda over 2-- that's
the basic length.

01:10:13.800 --> 01:10:18.690
And here is 2 times lambda
over 2, so it's just lambda.

01:10:18.690 --> 01:10:21.814
And times n.

01:10:21.814 --> 01:10:26.160
This n is an integer--

01:10:26.160 --> 01:10:32.170
no longer the number
density anymore.

01:10:32.170 --> 01:10:41.350
So lambda over 2 times n should
equal to the thickness, d,

01:10:41.350 --> 01:10:46.300
or diameter automatically.

01:10:46.300 --> 01:10:48.930
So this is d.

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:52.950
And this gives me
lambda equals 2d over n.

01:10:58.025 --> 01:11:04.760
And that will give me
momentum equal h over lambda.

01:11:04.760 --> 01:11:12.960
And that's 2ndh--
wait, wait, wait.

01:11:12.960 --> 01:11:13.980
Sorry.

01:11:13.980 --> 01:11:17.250
So 2nh over-- no.

01:11:17.250 --> 01:11:17.940
nh over 2d.

01:11:22.760 --> 01:11:25.610
That's momentum.

01:11:25.610 --> 01:11:30.540
OK, now I say I come
to kinetic energy.

01:11:30.540 --> 01:11:31.670
This is a--

01:11:31.670 --> 01:11:32.660
I'm jumping, here.

01:11:32.660 --> 01:11:36.330
I say, OK, this wave
also has a momentum

01:11:36.330 --> 01:11:39.260
and has a kinetic energy.

01:11:39.260 --> 01:11:45.560
And this is quantized momentum
because that n must be integer.

01:11:45.560 --> 01:11:48.200
n equals 1, 2--

01:11:48.200 --> 01:11:49.340
right?

01:11:49.340 --> 01:11:55.010
So now the energy, En, is--

01:11:55.010 --> 01:11:57.770
if I do the classical,
it's mv squared.

01:12:00.320 --> 01:12:04.700
And I write that into
momentum squared--

01:12:04.700 --> 01:12:08.510
2m-- because the momentum is mv.

01:12:08.510 --> 01:12:10.710
I put the momentum in.

01:12:10.710 --> 01:12:18.770
So I will get 8md squared and
n squared times h squared.

01:12:18.770 --> 01:12:24.680
So now, suddenly, the
energy of this particle

01:12:24.680 --> 01:12:28.743
discontinues because of
the wave requirement.

01:12:31.530 --> 01:12:36.580
So I need to ask,
is this important?

01:12:36.580 --> 01:12:43.380
So that clearly depends on,
what's the m, and what's the n?

01:12:43.380 --> 01:12:44.700
If the m and--

01:12:44.700 --> 01:12:46.290
say, what's the m
and what's the d?

01:12:46.290 --> 01:12:52.730
If m and d are large, the
energy separation is very small.

01:12:52.730 --> 01:12:58.190
If they are small, this
becomes very important.

01:12:58.190 --> 01:13:01.290
How important it is?

01:13:01.290 --> 01:13:03.710
So we need to have,
again, order of magnitude,

01:13:03.710 --> 01:13:08.480
even though I always give
you very small numbers,

01:13:08.480 --> 01:13:11.930
but I want you to be
proficient in those numbers.

01:13:11.930 --> 01:13:15.480
You can choose
whatever unit you use.

01:13:15.480 --> 01:13:20.480
So let me first give order
of magnitude of kBT, right?

01:13:20.480 --> 01:13:25.490
We said everything is equal
partition and 1/2 kBT.

01:13:25.490 --> 01:13:28.610
So kBT is good
number to remember.

01:13:28.610 --> 01:13:29.620
How large is kBT?

01:13:32.720 --> 01:13:41.870
kBT is a 1.38 10 to the minus
23rd joule Kelvin and times 300

01:13:41.870 --> 01:13:43.490
Kelvin.

01:13:43.490 --> 01:13:44.675
And this gives me--

01:13:59.730 --> 01:14:02.310
let me do that by heart.

01:14:02.310 --> 01:14:18.330
OK, it's 4.16 10 to
the minus 21 joule.

01:14:18.330 --> 01:14:20.190
You see, I don't
remember this number.

